WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.470
SPEAKER: Hello, and welcome.

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:04.560
During this session, we're going
to present an in-depth demo,

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.720
where we will build the
necessary infrastructure

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.120
to support a fairly
sophisticated scenario for EDA

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.670
workload Burst case,
from customers on on-prem

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.440
to Google Cloud.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.079
There is a lot to cover,
so let's get started.

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:17.850
The idea behind
this demonstration

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.590
is to showcase
the Cloud benefits

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:21.720
for EDA customers
that consider to start

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:22.890
their journey to Cloud.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.260
Specifically, to
highlight the features

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.830
such as Cloud elasticity,
flexibility, and scalability,

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:31.630
and the real value that Cloud
can bring to these companies,

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:33.840
which is a shorter
time to market.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.300
The ability to access
much larger resource

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.300
pools in the Cloud
enables engineering teams

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.300
to compress the total time it
takes to complete their jobs.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.760
And with EDA workloads
running in the Cloud,

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.940
these teams can perform more
design iterations per day,

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:51.300
and get these designs out
to the market much faster.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.790
We are going to start off by
presenting this setup we've

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:55.450
used to create this demo.

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:57.660
We've tried to build a
simplified architecture,

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.800
and have tried to be as close
as possible to real world

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.110
EDA architectures used today
by some of our customers,

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.210
and to run a flow
that resembles the EDA

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.160
Burst to Cloud scenario.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.990
This demo consists
of seven steps,

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.600
and we will go over each
one before diving in.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.850
Going to start with on-prem
system preparations.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.430
From on-prem Worker, we
will install and configure

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.500
the open beta, which
is an open-source,

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.360
general-purpose,
multi-threaded [? many ?]

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.390
core processor and
framework environment.

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.600
In our case, we're exploiting
it as an infrastructure that

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:31.380
is used to configure and run
simulations and, in particular,

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.080
will serve as a source for
configuration files that

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.200
will be used for
regression runs in GCP.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.140
Next, we're going to deploy
to NetApp CVO instances

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.090
in GCP using Terraform,
and configure

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.760
the FlexCache relationships
between on-prem and GCP

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:46.230
environments.

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:50.070
We will configure a NetApp CVO1,
to act as both high performance

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.050
storage for the
workloads in the Cloud

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:54.510
and as a cache for open
[INAUDIBLE] configuration files

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:56.520
from on-premises.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.340
To an EDA workload in the
Cloud, the configuration files

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:00.600
appear to be local.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.330
And there is no need to mirror
all the tools and libraries

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:04.140
to the Cloud.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:07.620
Get just the data you need,
where and when you need it.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:09.900
We will configure the
NetApp CVO2 instance

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.570
as a target for the Cloud
regression run results.

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:15.060
And by creating a reverse
cache relationship,

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.490
these results appear to
be local to engineers

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:19.530
working on on-prem.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:22.260
During this step, we are going
to create a SLURM cluster.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.900
Later on, we will use
special arguments that

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.910
are provided to the
regression run command,

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:30.210
and launch each test
in a SLURM [INAUDIBLE],,

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:33.330
allowing for tests to
be run in parallel.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:35.670
Here, we will run
two regression runs.

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:37.890
First we'll be using
10 GCP Workers,

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:40.590
and the second will
use 20 GCP Workers.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.360
The goal will be to
present the effect

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.550
of compute scaling and
the FlexCache technology

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:46.320
implementation.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.540
Next, we're going to
present one of the benefits

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:49.860
behind the FlexCache.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.710
We will showcase the ability
to access the same files

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:55.110
from both on-prem
and GCP Environments

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.450
by going over the
regression runs results

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.790
from both locations.

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:02.250
Going forward we will
demonstrate a case

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:04.200
where this regression
runs results

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.510
can be copied to Google
Cloud Storage for later

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:08.910
on analysis or archiving.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:12.330
And the last step will be
the GCP Environment clean up.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:15.360
We will delete it all and
remain with the regression run

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.280
results in GCS.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.910
So let's dive into the demo.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:22.350
As it was presented
before, we're

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:25.440
starting with on-prem
environment configuration.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.990
As you can see here, we're using
the NetApp hardware storage

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:30.180
system.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:32.040
From the on-prem
Worker, pre-mounted

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:34.410
to the on-prem volume, we
are installing the open pit

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.470
environment, while created
configuration files

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:40.335
will be used as a source for the
future regression runs in GCP.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:49.700
We switch over to
the GCP environment,

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:53.120
where we will deploy to NetApp
CVO instances using Terraform,

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.480
followed by the FlexCache
relationships configuration.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:58.910
We're starting with
the clear environment.

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:01.160
And through connection
to the Bastion host,

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:03.410
we are verifying the
correctness of [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:06.230
from configuration and starting
the deployment process.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:24.920
After executing the
deployment, the progress

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:27.470
can be monitored through NetApp
Cloud management interface

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:29.880
And once it completes, we've
got the relevant information

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:31.880
that will be used in the
next steps of the demo.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:47.590
By refreshing Google
Cloud console window,

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:51.580
we can see two new GCE
instances are created.

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:53.320
Having completed
the CVOs deployment,

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:55.400
we are going right in and
creating the FlexCache

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:56.380
relationships.

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:58.090
First, we will
create the connection

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:00.310
where the on-prem volume
will serve as a source,

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:03.737
and the CVO1 will be configured
with the FlexCache volume.

00:05:03.737 --> 00:05:05.320
To speed up the
configuration process,

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:07.030
we will use a simple
pattern script

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:09.550
that utilizes the
NetApp on-top REST API

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:11.260
comments to preparing
the [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:12.910
to FlexCache relationship.

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:15.400
We insert the required
information about both source

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:17.800
and target NetApp systems,
and execute the script.

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:22.150
Once it's done,
we are continuing

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:25.060
with what is called a Burst
caching configuration, where

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:27.460
the CVO2 volume will
be used as a source,

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:29.650
and the on-prem NetApp
system will be configured

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:31.510
with the FlexCache volume.

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:33.940
We will use the same method
of executing the configuration

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:35.815
through the prepared
head-of-the-time script.

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:48.060
We can verify the
successful configuration

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:50.220
by accessing the on-prem
Worker and trying

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:53.325
to mount it to created-a minute
ago on-prem FlexCache volume.

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:02.510
Having enabled the
FlexCache relationships,

00:06:02.510 --> 00:06:05.600
we're heading in deploying
the SLURM Workload Manager.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:07.940
This manager will be
used to orchestrate over

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:09.980
the regression runs in GCP.

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:11.690
The link to SLURM
deployment files

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:14.120
can be found in the comments
below or in the demo

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:16.340
walkthrough documentation.

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:18.470
The SLURM will be
deployed using Terraform.

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:20.630
So we switch over to
the configuration file,

00:06:20.630 --> 00:06:23.540
and update it with all the
relevant information required

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:24.830
for the deployment.

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:27.650
Again, all the information
can be found in the demo

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:29.660
walkthrough documentation.

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:32.880
It takes a couple of minutes for
the installation to complete.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:35.060
We can see the new SLURM
instances are created

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:37.890
in our project and it will
take additional 15 minutes

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:39.515
for the deployment
process to complete.

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:47.660
While waiting for the SLURM
deployment completion,

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:49.535
we're going to create
performance dashboards,

00:06:49.535 --> 00:06:51.650
using Google Cloud Monitoring.

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:53.495
The goal is to be
able to follow the IO

00:06:53.495 --> 00:06:56.030
Flow during the regression runs,
and present the NetApp CVOs

00:06:56.030 --> 00:06:59.610
read-write throughputs.

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:02.100
We can verify SLURM deployment's
successful completion

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:03.750
by connecting to the
SLURM Log In-Out.

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:05.730
It's mounted to
both NetApp CVOs.

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:08.745
We can access on-prem data
using FlexCache and SLURM

00:07:08.745 --> 00:07:13.290
nodes are ready to be
allocated to run the workload.

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:15.650
Now, we are ready to
execute the aggression runs.

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:17.390
First we will be
using 10 Workers

00:07:17.390 --> 00:07:20.330
and then we'll perform the
same, but scaling the Workers

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:21.800
from 10 to 20.

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.980
Each regression run
will include 46 tests.

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:27.290
The run will be started
from the SLUM Log In-Out

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:29.540
And once started, it
will require the access

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:32.180
to relevant configuration files,
which are currently located

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:34.310
at on-prem NetApp volume.

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:36.610
Once these files are
cached locally in GCP,

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:39.500
SLURM Batch job has started,
allowing for regression tests

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:41.300
to be run in parallel.

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:43.250
New regression run
results directories

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:46.010
created at CVO2 and 10
new computing instances

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:47.255
are created in our project.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:56.880
And now let's
monitor the IO Flow.

00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:59.610
Newly created Workers are
mounting to both NetApp CVO

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:02.160
instances, and reading
the run configuration data

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:03.810
through CVO1.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:05.940
Since the data is not
presented locally,

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:07.830
it is fetched from
on-prem volume.

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:09.390
Of course, all
that is completely

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:12.000
transparent to the workers
running the test in GCP,

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:16.282
and the configuration files
appear for them to be local.

00:08:16.282 --> 00:08:18.240
We can see that the Worker
CPU [? transition ?]

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.930
is getting higher once they're
starting to run the test.

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:23.520
And the test results
are written back

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:26.325
to dedicated volume created
at NetApp CVO2 instance.

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:54.370
Once we are done with
the first regression run,

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:55.930
we want to verify
that the results

00:08:55.930 --> 00:08:59.710
folder is accessible for both
GCP and on-prem environments.

00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:02.950
First, we are accessing the
results folder located at CVO2

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:04.330
from GCP instance.

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:07.450
And as you can see, there is
a list of the completed tests.

00:09:07.450 --> 00:09:11.320
Now, using the FlexCache, we can
access exactly the same folder

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:14.770
from on-prem Worker, and
can read any of the files

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:15.580
from there.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:25.760
Before we continue
to the next step,

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:28.700
we can refresh the Google
Cloud Console window,

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:30.500
and we can see that
all the 10 Workers

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:33.530
are deleted automatically by
SLURM upon job completion.

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:38.280
And now, we will execute
the same regression run,

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:41.280
but this time with 20
instead of 10 Workers.

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:44.010
The first step will be to
update the SLURM configuration

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:45.390
and validate it.

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:47.460
We're changing the
number of Workers

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:49.755
to be deployed from 10 to 20.

00:09:58.158 --> 00:10:00.200
[? End ?] validating, the
configuration is saved.

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:05.240
Starting a new job right now.

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:07.790
The new run starts
quicker, since the part

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:10.110
of the configuration files
already cached and located

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:10.985
locally in the Cloud.

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:21.860
Once a new job is started, we
can see that the new regression

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:24.710
run results directory
is created at CVO2,

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:29.720
and 20 new compute instances
are created at our project.

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:31.520
While monitoring
the IO Flow, we can

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:33.500
identify pretty much
the same behaviors

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:35.090
as we did with the first run.

00:10:35.090 --> 00:10:37.610
But this time, much
less data is fetched

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:40.520
from on-prem environment, since
it's already partially cached

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:41.930
in the Cloud.

00:10:41.930 --> 00:10:44.120
Workers maximum read
throughput is twice higher

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:45.530
than with previous run.

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:48.470
And while writing back the
results to CVO2 instance,

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:51.260
the overall max
throughput is higher.

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:54.510
All that happens because we
have now twice Workers running

00:10:54.510 --> 00:10:55.760
the same workload in parallel.

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:12.370
This run completes much
faster in comparison

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:13.610
to the first attempt.

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:15.370
The time savings are very clear.

00:11:22.870 --> 00:11:25.570
And again, as with previous
run, we can validate it.

00:11:25.570 --> 00:11:27.310
All the results
can be accessible

00:11:27.310 --> 00:11:30.610
for the further analysis
from both environments--

00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:32.939
GCP and the on-prem.

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:54.630
By refreshing all Google
Cloud Console window,

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:57.750
we can verify that all the
Workers are deleted again

00:11:57.750 --> 00:12:00.555
by the SLURM automatically upon
the completion of the test.

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:05.470
Now, when we have all
the results in the GCP,

00:12:05.470 --> 00:12:08.080
we can move it to a less
expensive storage option, where

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:10.750
it can be accessed later on
for further analysis or just

00:12:10.750 --> 00:12:12.160
being archived.

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:15.400
We're creating new GCS bucket
and copying both results

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:16.360
directories there.

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:30.690
Once copied, it can be accessed
through Google Cloud Console

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:32.475
or by any other available means.

00:12:54.370 --> 00:12:57.190
Having done all that, and
assuming that we currently

00:12:57.190 --> 00:13:00.160
don't need anything else from
our Cloud Infrastructure,

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:02.530
we can go ahead
and clean it all.

00:13:02.530 --> 00:13:04.570
After deleting the
FlexCache configuration,

00:13:04.570 --> 00:13:08.560
we proceed to deleting the
NetApp CVOs using Terraform.

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:11.320
While we're running that, we
can destroy SLURM deployment

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:13.630
as well, again, by
using the Terraform.

00:13:27.670 --> 00:13:29.530
Once everything is
completed, we can

00:13:29.530 --> 00:13:31.900
see that our GCP
project is empty,

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:34.780
and our regression runs
results are still remaining

00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:36.990
for access through GCS.

